{
  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Identify the rounding rule (d.p. or s.f.) and the required precision",
      "Find the critical digit (the one AFTER your last kept digit)",
      "If critical digit ≥ 5, round up; if < 5, round down",
      "For estimation, round each number to 1 s.f., then calculate"
    ],
    "title": "Decimals & Rounding",
    "content": "<p><strong>Decimals</strong> are another way to represent fractions and parts of a whole, using place value (tenths, hundredths, thousandths, etc.).</p><p>To <strong>round to decimal places (d.p.)</strong>, look at the digit <em>after</em> the required place. If it's 5 or more, round up. If it's 4 or less, round down (leave the last digit unchanged).</p><p>To <strong>round to significant figures (s.f.)</strong>, count from the first non-zero digit. The rules are the same — look at the next digit to decide whether to round up or down. For numbers less than 1, leading zeros don't count as significant figures.</p><p><strong>Truncation</strong> simply cuts off digits without rounding — useful when asked for a lower bound. <strong>Estimation</strong> means rounding each value to 1 s.f. before calculating, giving a quick approximate answer.</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>Round</strong> \\(3.4572\\) to 2 decimal places</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> 2 d.p. means keep two digits after the point: \\(3.45...\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Critical digit is the 3rd decimal: \\(7\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> \\(7 \\geq 5\\), so round up: \\(3.46\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(3.46\\)</p>"
  },
